window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-a5544ffc585ad19c"]={"key":"ny_main-a5544ffc585ad19c","group":"ny_main","uid":"a5544ffc585ad19c","rank":15,"title":"concierge mobile credential arrival template 001","occupation":"concierges","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You're Aleksy Sorensen, the concierge at Latchford Associates. Jacinta Kaczmarek needs a reusable communication tool this week, before Thistledown Logistics moves 240 employees into its headquarters while the building shifts from physical access cards to mobile credentials. During the five-day move, either system may apply, and some occupants will be unable to complete setup. Mahdi Demirci needs reception, facilities, security, and tenant-experience colleagues to use the same editable template; it should remain reusable if Silverbrook Supply or Thornbury Analytics is the tenant instead. Create `thistledown_arrival_communication_template.pptx` and put it at the top level of the working directory. Keep it to no more than ten slides. It needs audience-specific arrival messages for employees, visitors, executives, and service providers, plus a clear projection of lobby demand for the 48 employees scheduled per day under 60%, 85%, and 100% mobile-adoption cases and 5% and 15% setup-failure cases. Use labeled assumptions for any other arrival volumes, and make the uncertainty visible. One rare but serious situation is an urgent safety incident involving someone who cannot complete credentials; treat that separately from routine setup help. Credential troubleshooting and access authorization may belong to IT, Security, or tenant HR, so do not blur that boundary. Keep the deck practical to edit and reuse, and do not invent confirmed building rules, contact details, guarantees, or entitlements.\n\n---\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `thistledown_arrival_communication_template.pptx` — An editable presentation template containing audience-specific arrival communications, conditional credential guidance, exception boundaries, and a scenario-based lobby-demand projection. (Must be an editable native presentation, not a flattened image or PDF.; Must contain no more than ten slides.; Must distinguish confirmed information, assumptions, and unresolved local-policy fields.; Must cover employees, visitors, executives, and service providers.; Must address both the temporary dual-credential period and credential setup failure.; Must keep access authorization, credential troubleshooting, and tenant-status decisions within their appropriate functional boundaries.; Must not invent building-specific rules, contact details, guarantees, or entitlements.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/thistledown_arrival_communication_template.pptx","bytes":58658,"extension":".pptx","url":"assets/5f721726b5a0691e2cbe80cedbd4b2abd2adcf111ddd47ac933bc47ef3a56c51.pptx","sha256":"5f721726b5a0691e2cbe80cedbd4b2abd2adcf111ddd47ac933bc47ef3a56c51","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"=== 幻灯片 1 ===\nLATCHFORD\nASSOCIATES\nArrival\ncommunication\ntemplate\nTemporary credential transition\n& lobby-demand planning\nUse case: Thistledown Logistics headquarters move\nA reusable message set and planning aid for a five-day move while physical access cards and mobile credentials may both apply.\nDOCUMENT CONTROL\nTenant / move: [Tenant name] — [Move identifier]\nTemplate owner: [Role / team]\nVersion: [v#]  •  Effective date: [date]\nLOCAL\nBRIEF FACTS + DERIVED BASELINE\nConfirmed: Thistledown Logistics • 240 employees • five-day move\nPlanning assumption: even distribution → 240 / 5 = 48 scheduled employee arrivals/day\nCONFIRMED\nHOW TO READ THIS TEMPLATE\nGreen = confirmed brief fact  |  Amber = planning case  |  Blue = resolve locally / replace before sending. This communication tool does not replace local security, IT, emergency, or tenant policy.\nReusable for another tenant (for example, Silverbrook Supply or Thornbury Analytics) after all bracketed fields and planning inputs are reviewed.\nArrival communication template • editable document-control fields • not an authorization record\n\n=== 幻灯片 2 ===\nConfigure before sending\n02\nMake the uncertainty explicit—do not turn local decisions into implied rules.\n1. COMPLETE THE LOCAL FIELDS\nTenant / move: [Tenant] / [Move ID]\nMove dates / arrival window: [confirm]\nCurrent credential status: [confirm dual-system dates]\nRoutine setup route: [IT / support channel]\nAccess decision route: [Security / authorized channel]\nTenant-status route: [Tenant HR / employer contact]\nUrgent safety route: [applicable emergency or Security channel]\nReception / arrival coordination: [role / channel]\nLOCAL\n2. RETAIN THESE FACTS\n• The move covers 240 employees over five days.\n• Under even distribution, plan on 48 scheduled employee arrivals each day.\n• During the temporary transition, either a physical access card or mobile credential may apply.\n• Some occupants may be unable to complete setup.\nDo not add unconfirmed hours, ID rules, escort rules, contacts, or entry promises.\nCONFIRMED\n3. SEND ONLY AFTER CONFIRMATION\n[ ] Audience wording checked\n[ ] Host / sponsor fields added where needed\n[ ] Credential status and escalation routes confirmed\n[ ] Safety route reviewed locally\n[ ] Any non-employee volumes clearly tagged as assumptions\nKeep bracketed fields editable. Remove internal planning notes from recipient-facing messages if local practice requires it.\nPLANNING\nEditable template • replace bracketed fields before use\n\n=== 幻灯片 3 ===\nAudience messages I — employees and executives\n03\nCopy, complete bracketed fields, then send through the appropriate tenant communication channel.\nEMPLOYEE ARRIVAL MESSAGE\nWelcome to [Tenant] at [location / arrival point] on [date]. During this temporary transition, your confirmed route may use either a physical access card or a mobile credential—please follow the route provided by [tenant / authorized team].\nIf you cannot complete mobile setup, use [routine setup help: IT / support channel]. Reception can direct you to the applicable route, but cannot reset credentials or approve access. Do not share or borrow credentials.\nIf your credential or access status is uncertain, [Security / authorized access channel] handles access decisions; [Tenant HR / employer contact] handles employee or tenant-status questions. This message does not confirm entry, activation, or immediate resolution.\nNext contact: [arrival coordination role / channel]\nLOCAL\nEXECUTIVE ARRIVAL MESSAGE\nFor your [date] arrival, the current transition may require either a physical access card or a mobile credential. Please use the credential route confirmed by [authorized team].\nIf assistance is needed, contact [discreet arrival-support role / channel]. Credential troubleshooting follows [IT / support route]; access questions follow [Security / authorized channel].\nThis message does not create an exemption, bypass, or access entitlement.\nNext contact: [executive coordinator / tenant-experience role]\nLOCAL\nRecipient-facing wording • conditional credential language • no implied access entitlement\n\n=== 幻灯片 4 ===\nAudience messages II — visitors and service providers\n04\nArrival coordination is not the same as access authorization.\nVISITOR ARRIVAL MESSAGE\nYour visit to [Tenant] is scheduled for [date / time]. Please coordinate with your host or sponsor, [host name / role], before arrival; they can confirm the visit context.\nAt arrival, follow [local check-in / identification / escort requirement — confirm locally]. If your host is unavailable or arrival details need updating, use [host / reception escalation role].\nArrival assistance does not confirm access. Local requirements and any authorization remain subject to the applicable process.\nNext contact: [host or sponsor contact / role]\nLOCAL\nSERVICE-PROVIDER ARRIVAL MESSAGE\nFor [service / delivery / work], please arrive for the confirmed [appointment / work order / service request] with [accountable tenant sponsor / Facilities contact].\nUse [local arrival / receiving instruction — confirm locally]. Any loading, service-area, or restricted-area access requires the applicable local authorization; arrival alone does not provide it.\nIf operational details change, contact [Facilities / sponsoring team role]. Security access questions follow [authorized Security channel].\nNext contact: [Facilities / sponsor / work-order owner]\nLOCAL\nKeep host, sponsor, appointment, work-order, and local-requirement fields configurable\n\n=== 幻灯片 5 ===\nRoute the issue to the right owner\n05\nReception coordinates communication and arrival support; it does not decide authorization or eligibility.\nSituation\nReception / concierge action\nPossible accountable owner\nEditable escalation field\nRoutine mobile credential setup help\nDirect using the approved route; collect only the minimum needed to route.\nIT or credential-support team\n[IT / support channel]\nPhysical access or entry decision\nDo not approve, reset, bypass, lend, or disable controls.\nSecurity / authorized access function\n[Security authorization channel]\nEmployee / tenant-status question\nDo not determine employment, visitor eligibility, or tenant status.\nTenant HR / employer contact\n[Tenant HR channel]\nUrgent safety incident\nPrioritize immediate safety and escalate under local procedure; keep credential issue separate.\nApplicable emergency or Security channel\n[urgent safety channel]\nPRIVACY BOUNDARY\nDo not ask reception to collect or circulate passwords, credential secrets, detailed device information, or unnecessary employee-status details. Use role-based routing and the least information needed to direct the person.\nDECISION BOUNDARY\nThis template supports communication and planning; it is not a security policy, authorization record, or evidence of a right to enter.\nEditable template • replace bracketed fields before use\n\n=== 幻灯片 6 ===\nDaily lobby-demand model — definitions and assumptions\n06\nA planning aid for a five-day move, not a forecast, staffing approval, or authorization decision.\nBRIEF FACTS + BASELINE\nConfirmed brief facts: 240 employees over five days.\nPlanning assumption: 240 / 5 = 48 scheduled employee arrivals per day under even distribution.\nTotal people arriving in this model stays at 48 each day. A credential-assistance case is a subset / interaction—not another person. Alternative adoption and failure cases must never be added together.\nCONFIRMED\nPLANNING CASES\nMobile adoption cases: 60%, 85%, 100%\nSetup-failure cases: 5%, 15%\nFailure denominator = mobile setup attempts only.\nFormula:\nmobile attempts = 48 × adoption\nfailed setups = mobile attempts × failure rate\ncredential attention cases = physical-card route + failed setups\nDisplayed as expected daily cases to one decimal; not individual predictions.\nPLANNING\nLOCAL INPUTS (OPTIONAL)\nVisitor/day: [planning assumption]\nExecutive/day: [planning assumption]\nService provider/day: [planning assumption]\nNo non-employee volumes are included in the employee model because none were supplied. If populated, label each input as an assumption and add each person once to the applicable daily total—do not apply employee credential failure rates to them unless locally justified.\nLOCAL\nInterpretation: lower mobile adoption increases the physical-card route; higher setup failure increases routine credential-help interactions among mobile attempts. These cases show possible pressure points only.\nEditable template • replace bracketed fields before use\n\n=== 幻灯片 7 ===\nDaily scenario output — 48 scheduled employee arrivals\n07\nAll values are alternative planning cases per day. Expected interaction values are rounded to one decimal.\nMobile\nadoption\nSetup\nfailure\nScheduled\nemployees\nMobile setup\nattempts\nPhysical-card\nroute\nRoutine failed\nsetups\nCredential\nattention cases*\n60%\n5%\n48.0\n28.8\n19.2\n1.4\n20.6\n60%\n15%\n48.0\n28.8\n19.2\n4.3\n23.5\n85%\n5%\n48.0\n40.8\n7.2\n2.0\n9.2\n85%\n15%\n48.0\n40.8\n7.2\n6.1\n13.3\n100%\n5%\n48.0\n48.0\n0.0\n2.4\n2.4\n100%\n15%\n48.0\n48.0\n0.0\n7.2\n7.2\nREAD THE OUTPUT\n48.0 is the total people in scope each day.\nPhysical-card route and failed setup are planning workload components.\n*Attention cases = physical-card route + routine failed mobile setups. It is not a headcount and does not add people to 48.0.\nCASE\nUNCERTAINTY VISIBLE\nRange in this model: 2.4 credential-attention cases/day (100% mobile, 5% failure) to 23.5/day (60% mobile, 15% failure). Use the range to discuss where arrival support may be needed; do not infer queue limits, staffing ratios, or service-time guarantees.\nDaily planning cases • failure rate applies only to mobile setup attempts • no double-counting\n\n=== 幻灯片 8 ===\nFive-day planning view — keep people and workload separate\n08\nEven distribution is a planning assumption; replace it if the tenant supplies a confirmed arrival schedule.\nMove day\nScheduled employee arrivals\nLow attention case\n100% mobile / 5% failure\nHigh attention case\n60% mobile / 15% failure\nNon-employee overlay\nDay 1\n48.0\n2.4\n23.5\n[visitor / executive / service provider assumptions]\nDay 2\n48.0\n2.4\n23.5\n[visitor / executive / service provider assumptions]\nDay 3\n48.0\n2.4\n23.5\n[visitor / executive / service provider assumptions]\nDay 4\n48.0\n2.4\n23.5\n[visitor / executive / service provider assumptions]\nDay 5\n48.0\n2.4\n23.5\n[visitor / executive / service provider assumptions]\nUSE IN A TEAM HUDDLE\nReception, Facilities, Security, and tenant-experience colleagues can compare the daily baseline with the credential-attention range, then confirm local routes and practical coverage. This deck does not authorize staffing, access, or security changes.\nLOCAL\nIF THE ARRIVAL PATTERN CHANGES\nReplace 48.0 only with a confirmed schedule or a clearly labeled planning assumption. Recalculate mobile attempts and failures from that daily baseline. Do not use the five-day total (240) as a single-day lobby count.\nPLANNING\nEditable template • replace bracketed fields before use\n\n=== 幻灯片 9 ===\nRare, serious exception — urgent safety incident\n09\nTreat an immediate safety concern involving a person who cannot complete credentials separately from routine setup help.\n1. PRIORITIZE SAFETY\nIf there is an urgent safety concern, use [applicable local emergency or Security channel] immediately and follow local emergency procedures.\nDescribe only the minimum information needed to route the incident. Do not troubleshoot credentials as the response to the safety concern.\nLOCAL\n2. KEEP BOUNDARIES INTACT\nUrgency does not authorize an improvised access workaround. Do not lend or share credentials, bypass access controls, disable security, or admit an unverified person.\nAny access decision remains with the applicable authorized function; credential troubleshooting can resume through the configured route when appropriate.\nBOUNDARY\n3. RECORD / HAND OFF LOCALLY\nUrgent safety escalation: [channel]\nAccess authorization: [Security channel]\nRoutine credential help: [IT / credential support]\nEmployee / tenant-status question: [Tenant HR]\nReception role: receive, direct, and coordinate within local procedure—not approve access, determine eligibility, or reset credentials.\nLOCAL\nSafety handling must be confirmed locally before release; no local emergency contact details, response times, or building procedures are asserted here.\nSeparate branch: urgent safety response ≠ routine credential troubleshooting ≠ access authorization\n\n=== 幻灯片 10 ===\nPeer-review quick check\n10\nUse this final slide to validate a completed version before distribution.\nMESSAGE CHECK\n[ ] Employee message names both possible credential routes and the setup-failure route\n[ ] Visitor message names a host / sponsor and configurable local requirements\n[ ] Executive message is discreet, conditional, and has no exemption\n[ ] Service-provider message names a sponsor / appointment / work order and does not imply restricted-area access\n[ ] Each message has a configured next contact\nLOCAL\nMODEL CHECK\n[ ] 240 employees / five days / 48 per day is retained or the change is labeled\n[ ] Adoption: 60%, 85%, 100% cases shown\n[ ] Failure: 5%, 15% cases shown\n[ ] Failure applies to mobile setup attempts\n[ ] Assistance is not counted as another arrival\n[ ] Other volumes, if used, are labelled assumptions\nPLANNING\nGOVERNANCE CHECK\n[ ] Local contacts and requirements completed\n[ ] Routine support / access / tenant-status / urgent-safety routes are distinct\n[ ] No passwords, credential secrets, or unnecessary personal data requested\n[ ] No promises of entry, activation, service time, capacity, or staffing level\n[ ] Template owner, version, effective date, tenant, and move ID updated\nBOUNDARY\nFinal use limitation: This editable template supports clear communication and lobby-demand planning. It does not replace local policy or make an authorization decision.\nEnd of template • retain a completed copy under local document-control practice","truncated":false,"note":"PPTX 共10页的文字提取；不还原位置、动画和版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"r01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted artifact is named exactly thistledown_arrival_communication_template.pptx.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Submitted filename and package structure.","grader_hint":"Check the top-level submitted file name and confirm it is a valid PPTX package."},{"rubric_item_id":"r02","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The presentation remains meaningfully editable, with native text and editable fields rather than a deck made only of flattened screenshots.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"PPTX object and text structure.","grader_hint":"Inspect several slides for selectable text, editable labels, and replaceable message or assumption fields."},{"rubric_item_id":"r03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck contains no more than ten slides.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Presentation slide count.","grader_hint":"Count the slides in the PPTX package and verify the count is at or below ten."},{"rubric_item_id":"r04","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck functions as a reusable template by providing clearly identifiable fields or prompts for adapting the tenant, dates, arrival details, credential status, contacts, assumptions, and audience wording.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Editable placeholders, labels, and message fields.","grader_hint":"Look for fields that a future tenant team could replace without redesigning the deck; generic prose alone is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"r05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck has distinct communication content for employees, visitors, executives, and service providers.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Audience labels and corresponding message blocks.","grader_hint":"Confirm that all four audiences are separately named and that each has audience-specific wording rather than a single generic message."},{"rubric_item_id":"r06","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The employee message explains that either a physical card or a mobile credential may apply during the transition and uses conditional wording rather than presenting one route as settled.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Employee message text.","grader_hint":"Check that both credential modes are represented and that the message tells the employee to follow the applicable or confirmed route."},{"rubric_item_id":"r07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The employee message includes a usable response for an employee who cannot complete credential setup, without treating the failure as proof that access must be granted.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Employee setup-failure wording and escalation field.","grader_hint":"Look for a separate setup-failure branch, a clear point of contact or configurable escalation field, and no implied automatic admission."},{"rubric_item_id":"r08","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The employee communication does not promise entry, successful activation, or immediate resolution when credential status is uncertain.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Employee message language.","grader_hint":"Search for conditional terms and inspect whether any unconditional access promise contradicts the stated uncertainty."},{"rubric_item_id":"r09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The visitor message makes host or sponsor coordination part of the arrival context and leaves local check-in or identification requirements configurable rather than inventing a universal building rule.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Visitor message and configurable fields.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the visitor is linked to a host or sponsor and that any ID, check-in, or escort language is qualified or represented as a local field."},{"rubric_item_id":"r10","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The visitor communication does not imply that an unannounced walk-in will receive access or that host confirmation is unnecessary.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Visitor message text.","grader_hint":"Review the visitor branch for unsupported walk-in or guaranteed-entry claims."},{"rubric_item_id":"r11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The executive message is appropriately concise and discreet while still making the credential uncertainty and available arrival-support route clear.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Executive message block.","grader_hint":"Assess whether the wording is suitable for an executive audience without omitting the material access contingency."},{"rubric_item_id":"r12","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The executive message does not create a special access entitlement, bypass, or exemption merely because the recipient is an executive.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Executive message text.","grader_hint":"Check that executive status does not replace credential or authorization requirements."},{"rubric_item_id":"r13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The service-provider message connects arrival to an accountable sponsor, appointment, work order, or equivalent operational confirmation and leaves site-specific requirements adaptable.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Service-provider message and fields.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the message distinguishes a service provider from a visitor and includes an accountable business or facilities connection."},{"rubric_item_id":"r14","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The service-provider communication does not assume that a vendor may enter a loading area, service area, or restricted space solely because they have arrived.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Service-provider message wording.","grader_hint":"Look for authorization or confirmation language instead of automatic access to operational areas."},{"rubric_item_id":"r15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Each audience message identifies an appropriate next contact, owner, or configurable escalation destination without fabricating a person or phone number.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Contact and escalation fields in each audience section.","grader_hint":"Verify that all four audiences have a usable ownership path, expressed as a placeholder or role where no confirmed contact was supplied."},{"rubric_item_id":"r16","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The four messages use audience-appropriate, plain language that a recipient could act on without asking the template author to interpret it.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered audience message content.","grader_hint":"Judge clarity, tone, actionability, and whether jargon or internal process language would obstruct the intended recipient."},{"rubric_item_id":"r17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck visibly distinguishes confirmed facts, planning assumptions, and unresolved local decisions.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Labels, legend, or structured fields throughout the deck.","grader_hint":"Check whether a reader can tell which statements come from the brief, which are modeling assumptions, and which must be confirmed locally."},{"rubric_item_id":"r18","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck explicitly describes the temporary dual-system condition involving physical access cards and mobile credentials.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Transition guidance and scenario content.","grader_hint":"Search for both credential modes and verify that the transition is treated as concurrent or conditional rather than as a completed replacement."},{"rubric_item_id":"r19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck does not present unprovided building rules, named contacts, phone numbers, access hours, identification requirements, or entitlements as confirmed facts.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"All deck text and data fields.","grader_hint":"Compare concrete operational claims against the request; unsupported local details must either be absent or clearly marked as placeholders or assumptions."},{"rubric_item_id":"r20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The demand model includes distinct 60%, 85%, and 100% mobile-adoption scenarios.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scenario table or model labels.","grader_hint":"Verify that all three requested adoption rates appear as separate scenarios and are not collapsed into one average."},{"rubric_item_id":"r21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The demand model includes distinct 5% and 15% setup-failure scenarios.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scenario table or model labels.","grader_hint":"Verify that both requested failure rates are represented and their effect is visible."},{"rubric_item_id":"r22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The model uses the five-day move-in horizon and correctly represents 240 employees as 48 scheduled employee arrivals per day under an even-distribution assumption.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Model assumptions and calculations.","grader_hint":"Check the stated horizon and confirm that the daily baseline is derived from 240 divided by five, with any alternative distribution explicitly labeled."},{"rubric_item_id":"r23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The model states or makes inferable the denominator for setup failure, and applies the failure rate consistently to the population it defines rather than silently applying it to every arrival.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Formula, notes, or scenario definitions.","grader_hint":"Determine whether failure is calculated against mobile setup attempts, all employees, or another defined group; award credit only if the choice is explicit and consistently used."},{"rubric_item_id":"r24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Scenario outputs are arithmetically consistent with the stated population, adoption rates, failure rates, and rounding convention.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Displayed formulas, tables, or recomputable values.","grader_hint":"Recalculate the displayed scenario values from the deck's own assumptions; allow only rounding differences that the deck explains."},{"rubric_item_id":"r25","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck separates total people arriving from the subset likely to need credential assistance or an exceptional intervention.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Demand definitions and scenario outputs.","grader_hint":"Confirm that failed setups are not mislabeled as additional people and that the model defines what its lobby-demand measure represents."},{"rubric_item_id":"r26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The model does not add the same employee to multiple credential scenarios or count an assistance interaction as a second employee arrival.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scenario structure and totals.","grader_hint":"Inspect whether cases are alternatives and whether subtotals reconcile without double-counting people or scenario branches."},{"rubric_item_id":"r27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Any visitor, executive, or service-provider volume used in the lobby projection is visibly labeled as an assumption rather than as a confirmed Thistledown fact.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Demand model assumptions.","grader_hint":"Check every non-employee input for a source label such as planning assumption, placeholder, or to be confirmed."},{"rubric_item_id":"r28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The projection is useful across the move-in period, showing daily or otherwise time-bounded implications rather than only an undifferentiated five-day total.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Time-based demand presentation.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the five-day horizon is operationally visible through daily values, a daily baseline, or an explicitly justified time aggregation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The scenario presentation makes clear that adoption and failure rates are uncertain planning cases, not predictions of actual individual behavior.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scenario labels, notes, and interpretation.","grader_hint":"Look for case labels and explanatory language that prevents readers from treating modeled rates as confirmed outcomes."},{"rubric_item_id":"r30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck interprets how changes in adoption and setup failure affect credential-related lobby workload instead of displaying numbers without operational meaning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Model commentary or callouts.","grader_hint":"Verify that the deck explains the direction and practical significance of the scenario differences."},{"rubric_item_id":"r31","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"Reception, facilities, security, and tenant-experience teams could use the projection to anticipate pressure points while recognizing that it is a planning aid rather than an authorization decision.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered projection and operational interpretation.","grader_hint":"Judge whether the model leads to a usable preparation conversation without pretending to approve staffing, access, or security changes."},{"rubric_item_id":"r32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck does not invent a lobby capacity, staffing ratio, queue threshold, or guaranteed service time that was not supplied.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Demand-planning content.","grader_hint":"Unsupported operational thresholds must be absent or clearly marked as proposed assumptions requiring confirmation."},{"rubric_item_id":"r33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The urgent safety incident involving an individual who cannot complete credentials is visibly separated from routine setup assistance.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Exception section or distinct scenario treatment.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the rare severe case has its own treatment and is not buried as another ordinary credential-help example."},{"rubric_item_id":"r34","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The safety exception does not authorize bypassing access controls, lending credentials, disabling security, or admitting an unverified person.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Safety-exception wording.","grader_hint":"Inspect the exception for an explicit boundary against improvised access workarounds."},{"rubric_item_id":"r35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The safety exception directs immediate safety concerns to the applicable emergency or security channel while keeping the communication template free of fabricated local contact details.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Exception escalation language and contact placeholder.","grader_hint":"Award credit when the deck prioritizes safety and escalation, with the actual local route represented as a configurable field if unknown."},{"rubric_item_id":"r36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck distinguishes routine credential troubleshooting from access authorization and from emergency response.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Workflow boundary statements and scenario branches.","grader_hint":"Check for three conceptually distinct cases rather than one generic instruction to contact reception."},{"rubric_item_id":"r37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck identifies IT, Security, and tenant HR as possible owners of different decisions without asserting an unsupported local org chart.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Ownership and escalation language.","grader_hint":"Confirm that credential troubleshooting, physical access authorization, and employee or tenant-status questions are not assigned indiscriminately to the concierge."},{"rubric_item_id":"r38","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The concierge or reception role is not represented as having authority to reset credentials, approve access, disable controls, or decide employee eligibility.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Role-boundary statements.","grader_hint":"Search the deck for explicit role limits and inspect any suggested reception actions for overreach."},{"rubric_item_id":"r39","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck avoids asking reception to collect or circulate unnecessary sensitive identity, device, credential, or employee-status information.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Message fields and troubleshooting guidance.","grader_hint":"Check that the template uses role-based routing and minimal information rather than requesting passwords, credential secrets, or detailed personal data."},{"rubric_item_id":"r40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The communication guidance does not present itself as a security policy or as evidence that any recipient is legally or contractually entitled to enter.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Scope statements and audience messages.","grader_hint":"Look for an appropriate limitation that the deck supports communication and planning but does not replace local security or tenant policy."},{"rubric_item_id":"r41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"Claims in the deck remain supported by the brief or are explicitly marked as assumptions, placeholders, or items requiring local confirmation.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Complete rendered deck.","grader_hint":"Review concrete claims about timing, access, contacts, check-in, and service levels for unsupported certainty."},{"rubric_item_id":"r42","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The template contains configurable ownership or escalation fields for routine setup help, access decisions, and urgent safety matters.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Editable escalation fields.","grader_hint":"Confirm that the deck can be populated with local role names or channels without requiring the author to rewrite the whole message set."},{"rubric_item_id":"r43","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck includes practical document-control information such as a template owner, version or effective-date field, and tenant or move identifier field.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Document-control area.","grader_hint":"Look for editable metadata that supports reuse and prevents an old transition message from being mistaken for the current one."},{"rubric_item_id":"r44","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The deck has consistent labels and visual hierarchy separating audience messages, scenario assumptions, demand outputs, and exception boundaries, with no materially clipped or unreadable content.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered slides.","grader_hint":"Render the deck and inspect readability, hierarchy, table legibility, and whether users can locate each major content type quickly."},{"rubric_item_id":"r45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"As a whole, the deck is credible and ready for peer review: it is concise, reusable, audience-sensitive, numerically coherent, explicit about uncertainty, and careful not to overstep security, IT, or tenant-HR authority.","weight":4.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":true,"evidence":"Rendered presentation and editable source.","grader_hint":"Evaluate the complete artifact as a subject-matter reviewer would, including practical usability and whether its wording could safely be reused with another tenant."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"An enterprise concierge is preparing a reusable arrival-communication and lobby-demand planning tool for a large tenant move during a temporary transition between physical and mobile credentials.","difficulty_sources":["judgment_load=4: material ambiguity about conditional access messaging, exception handling, and functional ownership","reference_count=0: the practitioner must rely on concierge and workplace-arrival practice","transformation_depth=3: reconcile audience needs, model scenario demand, and synthesize an editable communication tool"],"authoring_plan":{"reference_files":[],"gold_artifact":{"filename":"thistledown_arrival_communication_template.pptx","construction":"Create the deck offline with native editable text, tables, and clearly marked replacement fields. Keep the presentation compact enough to satisfy the ten-slide cap while making the four audience messages, scenario model, exception treatment, and role boundaries easy to locate.","content_constraints":["Use only the supplied facts as confirmed: Latchford Associates, Thistledown Logistics, the 240-person move, five-day horizon, dual physical/mobile credential condition, and the specified adoption and setup-failure cases.","Treat Silverbrook Supply and Thornbury Analytics as reusable-tenant examples only; do not turn them into unsupported facts about the current move.","Create four genuinely distinct message templates for employees, visitors, executives, and service providers.","For every audience, use conditional wording for credential status, include an appropriate action or contact field, and pair practical guidance with a clear statement of what the communication does not guarantee.","For employees, include both the physical-card and mobile-credential possibilities and a separate setup-failure response.","For visitors, require host or sponsor coordination in the communication logic and keep identification, check-in, escort, and local-hours requirements configurable.","For executives, preserve concise and discreet wording without granting an access exemption.","For service providers, connect arrival to a sponsor, appointment, work order, or equivalent confirmation without implying automatic access to restricted operational areas.","Explicitly distinguish confirmed facts, planning assumptions, and unresolved local fields.","Build a scenario model using 240 employees across five days, yielding a 48-per-day baseline under even distribution. Include separate 60%, 85%, and 100% mobile-adoption cases and separate 5% and 15% setup-failure cases.","Define the failure denominator explicitly. A defensible model may apply setup failure to mobile setup attempts, but it must not silently apply the rate to all arrivals.","Keep total employee arrivals distinct from credential-assistance cases and avoid double-counting assistance cases as extra people or adding alternative scenarios together.","Any non-employee arrival volume must be visibly labeled as an assumption or placeholder.","Show the projection over an operationally meaningful time basis and explain what higher or lower cases imply for lobby workload without inventing capacity limits or approving staffing.","Treat the urgent safety incident as a separate rare/severe exception. The deck must prioritize safety and escalation without authorizing credential bypass, shared credentials, disabled controls, or unverified entry.","Mark the boundary between concierge communication and IT credential troubleshooting, Security access authorization, and tenant-HR employee or tenant-status decisions.","Avoid requesting passwords, credential secrets, unnecessary identity data, or detailed device information.","Do not fabricate phone numbers, named local channels, access hours, building rules, legal entitlements, or service guarantees. Use editable role or contact placeholders where needed.","Include reusable document-control fields such as tenant, move, owner, version, and date.","Ensure rendered content is legible and that the deck can be edited by a peer without rebuilding its structure."],"derivation":"Recalculate every displayed demand value from the deck's own stated assumptions. Validate the audience messages against the four audience-specific criteria, the dual-system uncertainty, the separate safety exception, and the IT/Security/tenant-HR boundary. Render the PPTX offline, inspect for clipping and readability, count slides, and confirm that all unsupported local details are placeholders or labeled assumptions."}},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"single_artifact","structure":"freeform"},"reference_files":[],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"arrival_communication_template_deck","filename":"thistledown_arrival_communication_template.pptx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation","description":"An editable presentation template containing audience-specific arrival communications, conditional credential guidance, exception boundaries, and a scenario-based lobby-demand projection.","required":true,"constraints":["Must be an editable native presentation, not a flattened image or PDF.","Must contain no more than ten slides.","Must distinguish confirmed information, assumptions, and unresolved local-policy fields.","Must cover employees, visitors, executives, and service providers.","Must address both the temporary dual-credential period and credential setup failure.","Must keep access authorization, credential troubleshooting, and tenant-status decisions within their appropriate functional boundaries.","Must not invent building-specific rules, contact details, guarantees, or entitlements."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"communicate_stakeholders","work_product":"form_or_template","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"closed_world_with_assumptions"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"concierges","sector":"real_estate_rental_leasing","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":3.5,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have a domain-expert reviewer verify concierge practice, audience distinctions, exception boundaries, and unsupported claims; use an independent structural review to recalculate the scenario model, inspect editability, and verify the slide cap.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["No building-specific security, IT, emergency, privacy, or tenant-HR policy is supplied, so local contacts, hours, identification rules, and escalation channels must remain configurable rather than asserted.","The requested adoption and failure percentages are planning cases, not observed operational data."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:02:42+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:02:42+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:02:42+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-17T08:02:42+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/a5544ffc585ad19c","declared_deliverables":["thistledown_arrival_communication_template.pptx"]};
